#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a276ef48-ee77-5113-bbb1-817ce6c31e75" :question/math-academy-id "e-3593" :question/problem "On which intervals is the function $y= f(x)$ below continuous?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/0a43ec719ede0b6c3b31fb585b52e514.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-2,1)$ and $(1,2)$. There is a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is already excluded.\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-1$, so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous in the intervals $(-2,-1)$, $(-1,1)$, and $(1,2)$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "7c67eb12-1841-5855-b6b6-4c2587c9dbd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3594" :question/problem "On which intervals is the function $y= f(x)$ below continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/f51fc708b4c8a9aaaec9baa0c6c07a4d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(1,7)$.\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=1$ and left-continuous at $x=7$. Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[1,7]$.\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-2,1)$, and $[1,7]$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "52452253-0dbc-5dbe-92c4-05f9e050a100" :question/math-academy-id "e-6161" :question/problem "Determine the intervals in which the function $y= f(x)$ below is continuous. You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/ded1e07424bbf156ab03101610734718.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-1$. Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[MATH: [-1,1).]$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$, $[MATH: [-1,1)]$ and $(1,\\infty)$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a950dcd4-ab41-526c-9ae3-c8fe471fcff4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6162" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/80b16a16a84dcd00a43c21b0c1cf96ac.png)\n\n1. $x\\in (-\\infty, -3)$\n2. $x\\in (-2,3)$\n3. $[MATH: x\\in [3,\\infty)]$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty,-3)$. It is continuous on $[MATH: (-\\infty, -2]]$ and this contains the interval $(-\\infty,-3)$.\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-2,3)$.\n- The function is continuous on $[MATH: [3, \\infty).]$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty)$ and also right continuous at $x=3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5e05ade-3560-5bcd-bc76-4a1da47d8fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/47dac620347d127e8b10c9678f09918f7ff4ccbc27cc30e224b99af38a52540b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263292} {:answer/id #uuid "866288ea-10f1-5135-a17e-c9b0f2a12899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04b4ed554ab43ef25caafe143aa2d4cdf280202f8a3995b10bb19e42f8ec5496.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263293} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa4bbc0-6b5a-536d-bcf9-3b14c9205e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/deae562b9b3cbbbf07c601c0560084686a2c14453a20dd7148846e2f7d9f623f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263294} {:answer/id #uuid "269d011a-b9b7-5e56-ba7c-3698df605510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263295} {:answer/id #uuid "a04f609a-ee17-561e-a7db-d92f6353b22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/cb326fe00fffc0f53f396ecca4975b7ad215d3e05821cfb9b1a365657e2ba97f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "269d011a-b9b7-5e56-ba7c-3698df605510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263295} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d21171-d578-57ea-82cc-70f7335019a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263291}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de2feaa-ecdf-5b6a-9c7c-51389b44cfed" :question/math-academy-id "q-104877" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-4,1)?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-4,1).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e49c6e1-4d55-5bee-a8e3-a4d8b895533f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263299} {:answer/id #uuid "485ca6f5-2099-5d35-9faf-4669ec15927a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263300} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3fffb7-d329-5f49-9031-290aae4aee3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263301} {:answer/id #uuid "57a0a417-e194-5969-ada8-bbb8dea4d316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263302} {:answer/id #uuid "f347298c-527a-5d74-a5b1-15b33fe1b22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e49c6e1-4d55-5bee-a8e3-a4d8b895533f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263299} :answer-field/id #uuid "08483342-8e51-5b2f-b963-30d881265e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2bf1f76-1005-59d7-a147-484f36ea43f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104881" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d6efb9c0bc8d7114c23ad3276303fe6898afcc4e27ad32db354f8e939d6f1c2e.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but this is already excluded. \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-3,1)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(-5,0)$ contain the excluded point $x=-3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(1,5)$ and $(0,\\infty )$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=4$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c1981c-b4c9-5bf6-9d41-54b028b9f1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263306} {:answer/id #uuid "5b35e066-f2c2-5d40-9c3d-2b43d81f330c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263307} {:answer/id #uuid "755741a2-1242-5d61-9b50-71d716f92b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263308} {:answer/id #uuid "4b167fde-e3e6-569e-be6a-3dd0b39aa4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263309} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6b7ef9-b0ca-5c3a-8ba2-6d2a9427eb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c1981c-b4c9-5bf6-9d41-54b028b9f1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263306} :answer-field/id #uuid "479fb131-cd6d-55ca-9993-e052da3555a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db8e7347-ce26-542d-a0dc-df7db098b3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104882" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d068feef885b3bb1ca3770a2d2648e9953f3ca9e1823111c507934b39ad71a76.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but this is already excluded.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-5,-1)$ \n\nNotice that the intervals $(-3,\\infty ),$  $(-3,5),$  $(-\\infty ,0)$  and $(-3,10)$ contain the excluded point $x=-1,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fd436e-939c-593f-bb98-76d1c9d8da32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/f13573a5bfe5661e1b9853ea559c88f48a887b3fa0618605b7f3a4c933645af3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263313} {:answer/id #uuid "da5e02fa-5e1a-5666-a0fd-38689a0be1fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/f0f65307224a5fea45530fd542be70d91039bc55aa2cd8b9f17537874c5170ae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263314} {:answer/id #uuid "f85e60be-847a-5453-91c1-0e9416e62231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d5cfd29b266bee94ed812edb71ac9804e385c9523341816be98ac278c088ef3b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263315} {:answer/id #uuid "be3e555e-576d-5656-8fe0-8fe35b2374df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263316} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cebba2-f0e3-5dc2-b80c-8fa9ced43e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5b2ca56f8c5c41015e6b0dc42311892becbb9f99233f5f9ddee951b993af95d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be3e555e-576d-5656-8fe0-8fe35b2374df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fb4569-b862-5f80-b0a6-961887bc3cde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10cb2f1-287e-54d9-a68e-50d79d0f8000" :question/math-academy-id "q-105181" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(1,5).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[1,5).$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $[1,5).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[1,4]$ because $[1,4]$ is contained inside the interval $[1,5).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $q(x)$ is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=3.$ So it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not defined at $x=4,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c25b8d-08de-5f88-a7d4-6386606a8894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d9b593075b4c09d73dcb28b77a94cd44d33f95ccc668a7f2fd5a26bf185453ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263320} {:answer/id #uuid "e69c7919-062e-52fc-aa70-25342928ba6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/105d87736460f2949a0cde4c7fe7cc105e72889312a529970d74080b14e3ffe8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263321} {:answer/id #uuid "22919f56-4710-5148-830d-2be158dc21da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/72e9c307e6cdea7b8496f48dbc2cc6894cbb7d2256a9d4b5d4bc43836643c140.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263322} {:answer/id #uuid "f372e498-7d36-5fc6-b297-55ea2171c84b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6c1a3827a38f51c479433b76ef4bfa7b9611e889c90fb44b0bf286582b24120d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263323} {:answer/id #uuid "c1418db5-b451-5428-b6f1-96ebb3ca2e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/94670d4b3cfe9d37d8c67e655c240db897362294999314ac5426ce5acb935c7c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e69c7919-062e-52fc-aa70-25342928ba6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/105d87736460f2949a0cde4c7fe7cc105e72889312a529970d74080b14e3ffe8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae8089ab-4e1c-5702-b2db-6f9decb4e524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13f8c420-bff7-5362-ad65-7f9dc2709f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-105394" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/105d87736460f2949a0cde4c7fe7cc105e72889312a529970d74080b14e3ffe8.png)\n\n \n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-1,2).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[-1,2).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $h(x)$ is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $[-1,2).$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$, and so is not continuous on $[-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8f57b5-b857-5443-9c53-45b3aa433381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/fb960ae354431f2da96637e4af9b67f1b05a19a8efef7a29c87fcbfd763d90bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263327} {:answer/id #uuid "d572e238-9efe-59ce-aac2-42282576f8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/3b29d25f5093ccbe9f3ace3076c81f37103c07f1f48cad16cbc0ee370695820c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263328} {:answer/id #uuid "f712d8b7-3811-5270-bf4e-bbac6f54a846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/380df9811d08cf8f60f479461de0f7b1eb71e93342e69a83682e18acd6e0b027.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263329} {:answer/id #uuid "301864f2-f0a1-5ec9-ae44-043e41ef411f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/feee0332de88587b13bf58e4f860d76483646f41676c0bb335b4a9d5b32b3218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263330} {:answer/id #uuid "88f0187a-3940-5076-acb9-027ccb3faab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/0121e04331b57e6b44e30c42bab437e897dce55be8af4a3819a81a8520c6a318.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "301864f2-f0a1-5ec9-ae44-043e41ef411f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/feee0332de88587b13bf58e4f860d76483646f41676c0bb335b4a9d5b32b3218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263330} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7b7af7-3028-575a-97fe-a2901a3fdace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f37c845d-39cc-5ed2-8317-fd82a20bf3e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105402" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/feee0332de88587b13bf58e4f860d76483646f41676c0bb335b4a9d5b32b3218.png)\n\nWe see that $p(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,-\\infty ),$ and since $[2,\\infty )$ lies completely inside $(-2,\\infty ),$ it is also continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=3,$ and so are not continuous on $[2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be3a6032-a8a8-5bdf-8df5-9c94ea3a6300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e6f54ad54f5b89f674469c29781d416f0d498855c6d788104d47a8119fa197e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263334} {:answer/id #uuid "9d90d996-5c5f-56b3-b4e5-ba3b0f3df9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263335} {:answer/id #uuid "5495dd0c-bfc0-5f7a-b998-f4c459a9a3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/24551c0a0d905070625c0d1ef76fa942dab6764c1540f7ed317eda533dca06d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263336} {:answer/id #uuid "37289a07-5ea1-5d3c-8214-4e125cffa24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/95587cfce50fd6e9c448bf3485dd98f7dd5bb401778b7a9fac939d356a112677.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263337} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0de68d-ee83-5dbb-902e-926a8fbb3a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/99e22a7485ab6e5b2a086418678d90160644814965aac3a3d1aede04e7cc6f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d90d996-5c5f-56b3-b4e5-ba3b0f3df9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263335} :answer-field/id #uuid "99759177-3d20-56ff-9903-5382769d1fc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19bb2540-93a4-5094-9d6e-6f7514d17932" :question/math-academy-id "q-105413" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png)\n\nWe see that $q(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ and since $(-\\infty ,2]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,3],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-1$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=0$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b610421d-b970-5cbf-8e45-22ab853cdf89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263341} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c32a-7b7b-5799-af30-11b1a9896635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263342} {:answer/id #uuid "d0633811-d841-5658-8eb3-140465ba26f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263343} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e672e-30fe-52a7-a4a1-722538b115f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263344} {:answer/id #uuid "985118e9-4b7d-5ec2-a131-0eacf3feb74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985118e9-4b7d-5ec2-a131-0eacf3feb74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263345} :answer-field/id #uuid "412cb687-fc68-5ada-a756-eca977d828c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3f87a1e-7af1-56ec-8bb5-254f0576f021" :question/math-academy-id "q-105418" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/b875f560c133e528e4d934ae2722d14c116fac4352f6cca5089f8ec726fe3660.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]$\n- $x\\in [-2,4]$\n- $x\\in [4,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-2),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,4].$ It is continuous on $(-2,4),$ and also right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=4.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[4,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(4,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e660ba2-21f2-5131-8015-7d3497b03d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263348} {:answer/id #uuid "589f972d-fc94-589a-be29-c7a54c1ac356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263349} {:answer/id #uuid "b04caa63-372b-5ee3-a6b9-5fa9426023a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263350} {:answer/id #uuid "43a55ca8-061a-5c50-a0eb-5164188fc8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263351} {:answer/id #uuid "c88f6be2-7a76-5f05-9029-c335410f12b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b04caa63-372b-5ee3-a6b9-5fa9426023a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263350} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d46998-f0fa-5796-b618-0587a303b5f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263347}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71a012b2-9b99-5389-ae49-c034c5fda9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/c75313fbb97784b27ea0c57068e7221e0f9416ac4857b70723fd7ca949d9aba7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and also left-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-3,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-3,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa5f3ae4-d5d9-5ff1-bfef-e19423239c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263355} {:answer/id #uuid "9deb6886-8c97-5952-b3f7-b4bb34366e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263356} {:answer/id #uuid "48452b01-a88b-52cf-9e03-fd0651dc80c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263357} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f23e81-eb2d-5888-b487-2e61c666507e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263358} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc9349d-be7d-5831-8f88-9899991392d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9deb6886-8c97-5952-b3f7-b4bb34366e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263356} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb4ba81-a5d6-5c96-93a4-2eaf749f545c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263354}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b5e7d86-6590-5293-af9e-465db1c9211b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918d5704-c936-55d5-8203-eac0ffb33b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076660} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0d0ca5-e6b9-5a9b-9739-4d0d30653d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076661} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4479b-f8c0-5b64-9dd7-acfb425f9f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076662} {:answer/id #uuid "004d454c-d47a-5a5b-a963-7444ffec7a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076663} {:answer/id #uuid "561a3430-7984-5ce3-8fad-90a652ccf2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561a3430-7984-5ce3-8fad-90a652ccf2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3688b07-cbb6-5b89-9be0-3e7661ec0fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076659}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9688522-541f-5cbf-8ad0-7cde48d13425" :question/math-academy-id "q-48293" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[- 1, 2]$?" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a972c906-1ba9-5370-a2ef-931f7869ea70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076672} {:answer/id #uuid "ae162c46-735d-5588-af6a-ea75b520d52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076673} {:answer/id #uuid "b5380aa0-0147-5f18-9794-596315569914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076674} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a52e50-a876-5bda-9cca-ec6c8d2e985b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076675} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0f75be-d250-594d-a782-086e5a073d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a972c906-1ba9-5370-a2ef-931f7869ea70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076672} :answer-field/id #uuid "66677f6d-342d-5ea9-960c-ce98fab85a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bbc454b-136b-559e-ae09-8d1685b0819e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48346" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48346.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n1. $[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 1]]$\n2. $[MATH: x ∈ [1, ∞)]$\n3. $x ∈ (-1, 1)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,1].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[1,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(1,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-1,1).$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and this contains the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03424df-5aea-5191-99c1-1cc0fa7a2289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/1326b043af92c5945a5fa679f7daa57eb269ae13edb6d3f9296b3a522678039c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263362} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4858bf-3342-5e79-a865-05e4599f361c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6c04be0224db916731c3f7b4e2093887ffd36a258b19c4112766125ff435704c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263363} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4fce77-e994-57c4-9221-5dd27bac5ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/49356dbd9984d40899b321698d641f200d1d28d498323aa88fdfdfac53ed7910.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263364} {:answer/id #uuid "59be55c1-cf73-526a-9065-a467e1272bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263365} {:answer/id #uuid "1cde514a-3ff7-5f96-b072-b996c29150c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/da6eb0e1cc28a0bef252e0d66bb232006f6a6e54142b27bb04401ca97025af30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59be55c1-cf73-526a-9065-a467e1272bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263365} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfd042a-8208-580b-b598-ff2d85c792c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c74056bd-b0fc-5dc2-914f-2bbc6b987726" :question/math-academy-id "q-48348" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png)\n\nWe see that $h(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ and since $(-\\infty ,-1]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,0],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-2$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-3$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce4394c-2e56-5ebb-8784-e1f49aa67d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263369} {:answer/id #uuid "cf03cec3-2928-549e-8e03-c67fe0f6975c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263370} {:answer/id #uuid "6adccafa-f4f3-51de-8168-e95002dddf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} {:answer/id #uuid "60b2a4f1-0ba1-5547-9b51-ba34d405f17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263372} {:answer/id #uuid "f724c74f-9b9d-5f79-b139-2a1dd1299753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adccafa-f4f3-51de-8168-e95002dddf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8ce9b0-6dba-5d74-928a-8627b951dce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263368}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfd92570-bd08-5565-9c64-935de2ba2369" :question/math-academy-id "q-76110" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b4a72e-cf61-5d7c-9875-89713655e535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263376} {:answer/id #uuid "fb17ef71-f1dc-5542-9d9f-b16910823fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263377} {:answer/id #uuid "0370ea43-5661-59db-b258-49a1d358b7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263378} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a09cba-4049-5743-ba0e-55de2726f6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263379} {:answer/id #uuid "9667c274-b2ae-537f-8e44-c348998ff678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b4a72e-cf61-5d7c-9875-89713655e535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263376} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6485ab-e4a6-57b3-ac2c-020209b5772a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263375}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54f43dcc-abdb-5395-9797-01d9daa06906" :question/math-academy-id "q-76124" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04dcf34ee71d47a340cf46334ba08ae2c010bf9c284fc79188ff13ff5d42b243.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-2,1),$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-2,1].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-2,1]$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-2,1]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[2,3]$ and $[2,4]$ contain the point $x=3$ at which the function is not defined, so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af0f37f-ecbb-5456-99f5-b08a6639ff67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-5,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263383} {:answer/id #uuid "44214d1a-a80f-5beb-8146-5b532d5e09e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "8500702a-318c-50a4-a11a-499484196874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263385} {:answer/id #uuid "b82aa7b8-9a7e-5161-9850-cc5ead98b512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263386} {:answer/id #uuid "522882f7-67bb-5666-9bb8-f3442f5d995c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44214d1a-a80f-5beb-8146-5b532d5e09e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3df0306-cbbc-59ef-8a6b-81fa5cf9eabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e5b666-ac66-5dd8-ae44-6b6ed7115689" :question/math-academy-id "q-76130" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5d90e9eb29955babde9d3af1ccd690b9d6b8fae6c07ad14da3867318b8487d90.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals  $(-5,0),$ and  $(0,2\\pi ).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0$ and at $x=-5.$  \n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-5,0)$  and  $[0,2\\pi ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[0,\\pi ]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,2\\pi ]$ and $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ contain the point $x=2\\pi ,$ but the function is not defined at this point, so it can't be continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $[-5,0]$ and $[-2,\\pi ]$  contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f5fb461-0212-52ce-80a1-4c1ca89b5e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/2c2038b962892960e2d198038f46c844540dc70f9341487a1fa3cc85e38584db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263390} {:answer/id #uuid "55d8aebb-159a-5a5f-b20a-d1e075279cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e032276bfe03fe9a8e6df08e361c0a27e556e7901fd3560e5ee649220ba47aee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263391} {:answer/id #uuid "83e54f39-8d89-5f7f-a87e-6702d0363ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6abe338528df6cf89c3738319f4d8be0b87eee3dfbace1667942fc81ebeb5c4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263392} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0d1b68-398e-5d97-b82e-0b7281edea75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263393} {:answer/id #uuid "180267af-9e62-50ff-95b2-5e21665fae69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/05c57e680f84093ea3740f206db05b239eb50ea0336b86b9c589d93497d90d9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0d1b68-398e-5d97-b82e-0b7281edea75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263393} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f53737-9fed-57e1-991e-c634e786aa01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "914562d3-5f5a-58ee-b3fa-4cdc5878b28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76154" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2),$ and $(2,6).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2],$ and $(2,6).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $q(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at some point in the interval $(0,2).$  So it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9243421-0a20-54f3-8f74-3e96b0ab3892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d146dcbae1741d150191f2848ed6d0b000a913bfc2eb603b459ebe19a6a656aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263397} {:answer/id #uuid "eeafc3d3-1f5f-537d-8f10-c3e726c8484e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/2baaf696411a0d3ab6f20bf4977c78f3076f9665f9cac965a37cdca9fb4e8387.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263398} {:answer/id #uuid "9aff30f0-8e1f-5f7c-87d0-18d9dc56c7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/221f7b64094910a5c1037e7071826f27f2ea0472ea93a612e9d38f3c46f5ad27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263399} {:answer/id #uuid "298dc4a0-d057-5a3e-a1c1-b2198bff2755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/07b072fd0a49d28fda59c356fe6e8c344d6093f20e23835d26bc386cdd973876.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263400} {:answer/id #uuid "7b30b367-ebe1-5390-99ed-dca39ba96ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/ddd5e77600b8e9e16f1e985f8a8a306a97e1a9086cde845458e0d06f983c250a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aff30f0-8e1f-5f7c-87d0-18d9dc56c7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/221f7b64094910a5c1037e7071826f27f2ea0472ea93a612e9d38f3c46f5ad27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263399} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff57f0e1-98a8-5eae-8052-d41612468187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b98ca02-7865-58d7-8fbf-1f3bb92e16af" :question/math-academy-id "q-76158" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/221f7b64094910a5c1037e7071826f27f2ea0472ea93a612e9d38f3c46f5ad27.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $q(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,2).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-2,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $g(x)$, $h(x)$, and $p(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee898617-59af-5881-a9f4-c7efe7eae6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263404} {:answer/id #uuid "bdca861b-9fe0-5d31-9f0c-32734a7d1bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263405} {:answer/id #uuid "f81599db-f76f-57e1-83da-75c1e0776f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263406} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e1cc1f-42db-5556-9a5f-9e28200bfbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263407} {:answer/id #uuid "7804e45c-a6b6-51cb-8830-4f970fcc2cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee898617-59af-5881-a9f4-c7efe7eae6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263404} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b4ad01-3db6-5023-98ab-19c284784f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263403}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "354f4c56-c212-5e79-a571-1e084a5fc978" :question/math-academy-id "q-76208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b28f2a35-fde7-5bfd-b956-9e20dc4fa25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263411} {:answer/id #uuid "0336fecb-61a9-5551-8dd6-5a336acbe14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263412} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed4b64f-be4b-5bdf-8b2d-241fde3223a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263413} {:answer/id #uuid "b354bee0-3206-5db6-aa68-31be2c26d012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263414} {:answer/id #uuid "300ec8df-aa7a-5b1a-adeb-773f1b1bfa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b354bee0-3206-5db6-aa68-31be2c26d012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263414} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab32362f-c25e-589c-bfe1-cad12ee169ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6acd958-af3c-5c3f-ba74-d4cadbc3229f" :question/math-academy-id "q-76218" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/186ac941080da34b7c149534a2e255852241b28855c0ef15db2beee2d692007a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,5)$\n- $x\\in (4,6)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,3].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,3),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,5)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(4,6).$ It is continuous on $[3,\\infty )$ and this contains the interval $(4,6).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
